{
  "dom_id": "step-q253559",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Consider the set $D=[\\sqrt{3},4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(D).$\nThe supremum of a set $D$ is the {{field-1}} of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $D$ is the smallest upper bound of $D.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $D$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge 4$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $D.$ Thus, the set $[4,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $D.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<4$ is **not** an upper bound of $D.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $D$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<4.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $D,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(D)=min[4,\\infty )=4.\n$$\n\nNotice that $4\\notin D.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $D$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge 4\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(D)=4\\notin D.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
